\paragraph{Data.}
Tier~A and Tier~B use generated data (\texttt{code/}).
Tier~C uses the CIFAR-10 training set \citep{krizhevsky2009cifar} (50{,}000 images) from the public Hugging Face parquet copy \texttt{uoft-cs/cifar10} (119{,}705{,}255 bytes; columns \texttt{img}, PNG bytes, and \texttt{label}).
The copy is pinned to dataset revision \texttt{0b27149}; train (50{,}000) and test (10{,}000) files are hashed in \texttt{results/raw/vit\_runs/c\_*/data\_provenance.json}. Images are normalized per channel with training-set statistics; 50 epochs are 9{,}800 updates.
Probe: the final online encoder is frozen, the mean-pooled embeddings of full (unmasked) images are computed, and a ridge classifier (scikit-learn \texttt{RidgeClassifier}, $\alpha=1$) is fitted on all training labels and evaluated on the whole test set.

\paragraph{Software and compute.}
Python 3.11.2, PyTorch 2.13.0 (CPU build), NumPy 2.4.6, PyArrow and Pillow; all runs use CPUs only, in a container with 8 CPU cores and 32\,GB of memory, at most 4 jobs at a time.
Before the final runs we measured an aggregate throughput of 8.49 training steps per second for the Tier~B model with 4 concurrent jobs, and 224--307 steps per second for the linear models; at that rate the roughly 89 nonlinear runs would have taken about 20 hours of wall-clock time.
These were measurements and estimates made for planning, not results of the study; the final runs were slower (see Design and run budget).
Each final ViT run uses 4 CPU threads; per-run wall-clock is recorded in each run's \texttt{summary\_seed*.csv}. The reduced grid (52 Tier~B and 18 Tier~C runs) was projected at about 56 hours with 4 concurrent jobs.
The 17 nonlinear runs committed so far took 8{,}846--11{,}089\,s each in Tier~B and 14{,}174--17{,}013\,s each in Tier~C, 53.3 hours of run time in total (sum of the per-run wall-clocks, \texttt{notes/tierB\_numbers.txt}); the totals of the final grid will be taken from the merged summaries.

